WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.838
Hi, friends. Gani here. This is Saying Sorry and Meaning It.

00:00:12.838 --> 00:00:17.675
Sit tall in your chair. Feet flat on the floor.

00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:20.578
Hands resting on your desk. Shoulders soft.

00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:23.480
Take one slow breath with me… and let it go.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:31.220
Sometimes we do something that hurts someone. A word. A grab. A broken thing.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:38.961
When that happens, there is a strong thing we can do. We can say sorry — and mean it.

00:00:38.961 --> 00:00:46.701
A real sorry has three parts. Let me show you each one.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:50.571
And first, hear this: nobody can make a sorry for you. A made-to-say sorry is just words.

00:00:50.571 --> 00:00:59.279
Part one: name what you did. Out loud. No hiding. "I knocked your tower down."

00:00:59.279 --> 00:01:07.019
Part two: say the sorry. Plain and whole. "I'm sorry." And then — stop.

00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:14.759
No "but" after it. "I'm sorry, but…" hands the sorry right back.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:21.532
Part three: make it right, if you can. Help build it again. Fix it. Clean it up.

00:01:21.532 --> 00:01:25.402
Words say it. Actions show it. A real sorry has both.

00:01:25.402 --> 00:01:33.142
Now the part many people forget. The person you hurt owes you nothing back.

00:01:33.142 --> 00:01:41.849
They don't have to say "it's okay." They don't have to hug. They don't have to be done being hurt.

00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:47.655
They might need time. Time is allowed. Your sorry was still worth saying.

00:01:47.655 --> 00:01:50.557
You did your part. Their answer is theirs.

00:01:50.557 --> 00:01:57.330
Name it. Say it plain. Make it right. That is a sorry that means something.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:02.167
Shoulders soft. Let your body rest.

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:06.037
Hands down. One more slow breath… and let it go.

00:02:06.037 --> 00:02:10.875
Eyes open. Look around your classroom.

00:02:10.875 --> 00:02:15.713
You're ready for what's next.
